From bb0bb2349aaddbdcb130508ce03a562d57ddce7f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: so1_sanctum <so1_sanctum@pm.me>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 23:22:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10/11] ptlc: add the secp256k1 point type and a fixed
 destination

A third point type locks to a compressed secp256k1 point T and is opened by
the scalar t with t*G == T, the lock Lightning's PTLC design describes,
offered directly because this VM can multiply a point. It is the lock the
zenon-zbtc prototype ran its BTC/ZNN swap on (patches/0003), moved into
this contract so that one contract carries every PTLC shape.

A scalar is a bearer secret: anyone who reads it from the claim's send
block could submit a ProxyUnlock to themselves before the contract acts.
So Create gains a destination argument. A point lock must name one, and
an unlock of such an entry pays nowhere else; a key lock may name one or
pass the zero address to let the signature choose. Embedded contracts are
refused as destinations because a contract send with no data would strand
the funds.

The RPC shape returns the amount as a decimal string, as htlc does, and
carries the destination. Errors: ErrInvalidPointScalar, ErrInvalidDestination.

Tests: the existing suite updated for the new Create signature (their
entry ids changed with the call data), plus ptlc_point_test.go for the
point type, the destination rules, expiry and reclaim, a key lock with a
fixed destination, and a NoM-to-NoM swap on two tweaked point locks.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
